WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.512
Encoding changes how software represents a variable.

00:00:03.512 --> 00:00:06.322
It does not change the underlying meaning.

00:00:06.322 --> 00:00:10.132
Nominal variables often use one-hot indicators.

00:00:10.132 --> 00:00:16.257
Ordinal variables may use ordered categorical dtypes or explicit ordinal codes.

00:00:16.257 --> 00:00:23.252
Numeric variables can remain numeric and may be scaled for methods that need comparable feature scales.

00:00:23.252 --> 00:00:31.102
Counts should remain conceptually counts even if a model later transforms them.

00:00:31.102 --> 00:00:33.787
The representation should support, not redefine, the concept.

00:00:33.787 --> 00:00:39.472
Preprocessing is not neutral: it tells software how to interpret the variable.

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:46.777
One-hot encoding avoids inventing rank for nominal categories; ordinal coding preserves a known order;

00:00:46.777 --> 00:00:52.137
scaling changes numerical representation but not conceptual meaning.

00:00:52.137 --> 00:00:59.788
In predictive work, learned preprocessing should be fitted only on training data so evaluation remains honest.
